<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Southern Cameroons</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1922">1922</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroons</a> was the southern part of the British Cameroons, a mandated and later trust territory administered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a> as its centre. It gained its own government in the 1950s. In the plebiscite of February 1961 it voted to join the former French Cameroun, forming the Federal Republic of Cameroon, the origin of today's English-speaking regions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/southern-cameroons.jpg" alt="Southern Cameroons" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_British_Cameroon.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the German territory of Kamerun was divided between a larger French mandate, Cameroun, and a British one made up of two adjacent territories, the Northern and Southern Cameroons. The British governed them from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, without joining them to it, through a Resident at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buea">Buea</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a> native authorities administered their peoples by their own traditions and collected taxes for the British. On 27 March 1940 students including Emmanuel Mbela Lifafa Endeley founded the Cameroons Youth League to oppose what they saw as the exploitation of their country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> approved the trusteeship agreement for the British Cameroons on 6 December 1946. In 1949 the Southern Cameroons was divided into the provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamenda">Bamenda</a> and Southern. Its thirteen representatives in the Eastern Nigerian House of Assembly at Enugu withdrew in 1953, and at a conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that year they asked for a region of their own. The territory became an autonomous region, and its parliament first met on 1 October 1954 with Endeley as Premier. John Ngu Foncha defeated him in the elections of 1 February 1959, and in 1961 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-cameroons">Northern Cameroons</a> voted to join <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and the Southern Cameroons to join Cameroun.</p><p>The territory became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> on 1 October 1961, and Foncha served as Prime Minister of West Cameroun and vice-president of the federation. After a referendum on 20 May 1972 a unitary constitution replaced the federal state, and the territory became the Northwest and Southwest Provinces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Many English-speaking Cameroonians felt unfairly treated. The first and second All Anglophone Conference, in 1993 and 1994, called for the 1961 federation to be restored, and pro-independence groups argued that UN Resolution 1608 had not been implemented and that the federal constitution of 1 September 1961 amounted to annexation. They renamed the territory Ambazonia, after Ambas Bay. In 2009 the African Commission on Human and Peoples' Rights found that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> had violated several articles of the African Charter and that the people of the Southern Cameroons were a people under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, but that the conditions for invoking self-determination had not been shown.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Southern Cameroons” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It consisted of two separate areas: the Northern Cameroons, bordering Bornu and Adamawa, and the <b>Southern Cameroons</b>, with Buea as its centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-cameroons">British Cameroons</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1961 the <b>Southern Cameroons</b> voted to join it, forming a federal republic, while the UPC insurgency continued into the 1960s.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-cameroon">French Cameroon</a></li></ul></section>
